# Introduction to the Keymaps of 99.lua

Welcome to **99.lua**, a Neovim plugin designed to bring powerful, interactive, AI-assisted development workflows right into your editor. By configuring your preferred AI providers (like Gemini or OpenCode) and utilizing standard Neovim keymaps, `99.lua` automates visual edits, search queries, work tracking, and model switching.

This tutorial guides you through the keymaps configured in `99.lua`, explaining their behavior, the modes in which they operate, and how to use them effectively in your daily coding routine.

---

## The Prefix Key

By default, all `99.lua` keymaps are prefixed with `<leader>9`. 
Since your `mapleader` is set to `<Space>` (spacebar) in `keymaps.lua`, all commands begin with:
`Space` followed by `9`

---

## 1. Core AI Interaction Keymaps

These are the primary keymaps you will use to communicate with the AI model for refactoring, writing code, and seeking assistance.

### Visual AI Edit: `<leader>9v` (Visual Mode Only)
- **Function:** `_99.visual()`
- **How it works:** Highlight a block of code in Visual mode (`v`) and press `<leader>9v`. This sends the visually selected code block directly to the AI as context for your instructions.
- **Why it's in Visual Mode:** To avoid accidentally sending an old or stale visual selection, this keymap is strictly mapped to visual selection mode.

### Cancel Active Request: `<leader>9x` (Normal Mode)
- **Function:** `_99.stop_all_requests()`
- **How it works:** Press `<leader>9x` in Normal mode to immediately abort any running AI requests. If you notice a prompt going off-course or making unexpected edits, use this to halt it instantly.

### Contextual Search: `<leader>9s` (Normal Mode)
- **Function:** `_99.search()`
- **How it works:** Starts a search-based prompt session, letting you search through your codebase context before sending queries to the AI.

### General Discussion/Chat: `<leader>9V` (Normal Mode)
- **Function:** `_99.vibe()`
- **How it works:** Launches a general-purpose chat or "vibe" session with the AI, allowing you to ask questions or discuss concepts without requiring specific code-editing commands.

---

## 2. Configuration & Provider Settings (Telescope Pickers)

When you want to change your AI model or switch to a different provider on the fly, `99.lua` provides seamless integration with Telescope.

### Select AI Model: `<leader>9m` (Normal Mode)
- **Function:** `require("99.extensions.telescope").select_model()`
- **How it works:** Opens a Telescope fuzzy finder listing all available AI models (such as Gemini Flash, Gemini Pro, Claude, etc.) so you can dynamically switch active models.

### Select Provider: `<leader>9p` (Normal Mode)
- **Function:** `require("99.extensions.telescope").select_provider()`
- **How it works:** Opens a Telescope dropdown to switch between active backend providers (e.g., Gemini CLI, OpenCode, Claude Code).

---

## 3. History & Task Management

Keep track of your previous prompts and organize larger development tasks.

### Open Previous Request: `<leader>9h` (Normal Mode)
- **Function:** `_99.open()`
- **How it works:** Press `<leader>9h` to open your previous request history. This is helpful for reviewing what was asked or resuming a past prompt.

### Set Work Item: `<leader>9w` (Normal Mode)
- **Function:** `_99.Extensions.Worker.set_work()`
- **How it works:** Sets a target work item or goal for the `99.lua` worker extension to track.

### Search Remaining Work: `<leader>9ws` (Normal Mode)
- **Function:** `_99.Extensions.Worker.search()`
- **How it works:** Searches and displays remaining work items and tasks that are left to complete.

---

## 4. Interactive Tutorial

### Launch Built-in Tutorial: `<leader>9t` (Normal Mode)
- **Function:** `_99.tutorial()`
- **How it works:** Launches an interactive, hands-on tutorial inside Neovim to help you master `99.lua`'s capabilities step-by-step.

